# The situation board.
#
# John: "in game i'd like to be able to make notes on the globe. pins with
# notes" and "i can put legend markers of suspected places and routes".
#
# The design doc describes the same screen from Cobra's side - every known site
# and route, each villain's last known location, each act project's clock as an
# estimate with a confidence bar that sharpens with intel. They are ONE system:
# what the player suspects and what intel has told him, on the same globe,
# drawn at different weights. Built apart they become two overlays that
# disagree about the same piece of ground.
#
# markIcons are GlobeMarkers frames, in a fixed order the engine indexes by:
#
#   confirmed, likely, rumour, note, unknown, route start, waypoint, area
#
# These are the icons drawn 11 Nov and wired at 28-35. They go through
# loadSpriteOffset, so they pick up the mod's own sprite offset exactly as
# markerIcon does - a literal frame number would point at vanilla art.
#
# markStaleMonths: a lead the GAME gave the player is drawn one tier less
# certain once it is this old. A pin the player placed himself never decays -
# he is not guessing about his own guess.
#
# markRouteColor is a geoscape PALETTE INDEX, not a frame: 144 is the gold the
# marker art itself is drawn in, so a route line and the waypoints along it
# read as the same pen. It is a flat colour on purpose. Globe::drawPath, which
# a craft's flight path uses, writes a SHADED COPY of the pixel underneath, so
# a route drawn through it disappeared over night ocean and the whole run read
# as three loose icons. Globe::drawMarkPath inks it instead, dashed, so it
# cannot be mistaken for a radar range either.
#
# PLACED WITH CTRL + RIGHT-CLICK, and hidden with keyGeoToggleMarks (m).
# Plain right-click centres the globe and always has; the board does not get
# to take it.

globe:
  markIcons: [28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34, 35]
  markStaleMonths: 4
  markRouteColor: 144
